-- Q1: hard / L3 — Produced-and-sold inventory split sourcing
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Cascade Manufacturing, a U.S. corporation, produces industrial valves entirely at its Ohio factory and sells them to unrelated buyers in Country K. Title to the goods passes at the buyer's warehouse in Country K. Cascade earned $4,000,000 from these sales. Under the production-and-sale rules of IRC Sections 861-863, evaluate how this income is sourced.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Allocated partly to U.S. and partly to foreign sources, because production occurs in the United States and the sale occurs outside the United States", "Entirely U.S.-source, because the goods are manufactured in the United States", "Entirely foreign-source, because title passes outside the United States", "Entirely U.S.-source, because Cascade is a domestic corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 863(b), when a taxpayer both produces and sells inventory, and the production and sale occur in different countries, the income must be allocated between U.S. and foreign sources. Cascade produces in the U.S. but sells abroad, so the income is split. Wrong (B): The production-only location controls for pure manufacturing sourcing, but when the sale occurs in a different country, a split allocation applies. Wrong (C): The title-passage rule applies to purchased inventory resold without production activity; produced-and-sold inventory uses an allocation method. Wrong (D): The seller's residence does not determine sourcing for inventory sales; the production-and-sale rules control.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Multi-income sourcing synthesis
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$GlobalSync Corp, a U.S. corporation, earns four income items during the year: (1) $200,000 in consulting fees for work performed entirely in Brazil; (2) $150,000 in rent from a warehouse GlobalSync owns in Chicago leased to a French tenant; (3) $100,000 in dividends from Komori Ltd., a Japanese corporation; and (4) $250,000 in interest on a note receivable from a U.S. borrower. Determine GlobalSync's total foreign-source income by applying the sourcing rules to each item.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$200,000, consisting only of the consulting fees", "$300,000, consisting of the consulting fees and the dividends from Komori Ltd.", "$450,000, consisting of the consulting fees, the dividends, and the Chicago warehouse rent", "$500,000, consisting of the consulting fees, the dividends, and the interest"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sections 861-862, services income is sourced where performed (Brazil = foreign), dividends are sourced by the payer's residence (Komori is Japanese = foreign), rent is sourced by property location (Chicago = U.S.), and interest is sourced by the borrower's residence (U.S. borrower = U.S.). Foreign-source total: $200,000 + $100,000 = $300,000. Wrong (A): This omits the dividends from Komori, which are foreign-source because the paying corporation is Japanese. Wrong (C): The Chicago warehouse rent is U.S.-source because the property is located in the United States, regardless of the tenant's nationality. Wrong (D): The interest from a U.S. borrower is U.S.-source, not foreign-source; interest follows the payer's residence.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Dividend from U.S. subsidiary to foreign parent is U.S.-source
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Sterling Industries, a corporation incorporated in Germany, wholly owns AmeriParts Inc., a U.S. corporation. AmeriParts declares a $750,000 dividend to Sterling. Sterling's tax advisor needs to determine whether U.S. withholding applies. Evaluate how the dividend is sourced under IRC Sections 861-862 and what that means for withholding.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The dividend is foreign-source because Sterling is a German corporation, and no U.S. withholding applies", "The dividend is foreign-source because AmeriParts earns most of its income from international customers", "The dividend is U.S.-source because AmeriParts is a domestic corporation, and it is subject to U.S. withholding", "The dividend is exempt from sourcing rules because it is an intercompany payment within a consolidated group"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 861(a)(2), dividends paid by a domestic corporation are generally U.S.-source income. Because AmeriParts is a U.S. corporation, its dividend to Sterling is U.S.-source and subject to U.S. withholding when paid to a foreign person. Wrong (A): The recipient's nationality does not control dividend sourcing; the payer's country of incorporation does. Wrong (B): The customer base of the paying corporation does not determine dividend sourcing under the general rule. Wrong (D): A foreign parent and its U.S. subsidiary are separate taxpayers; intercompany dividends are not exempt from sourcing or withholding rules.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — The 30% default statutory withholding rate
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What is the default U.S. statutory withholding rate on U.S.-source dividends, interest, and royalties paid to a foreign person who has not submitted documentation claiming a reduced rate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["10%", "15%", "21%", "30%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1441, the default statutory withholding rate on U.S.-source fixed, determinable, annual, or periodical (FDAP) income paid to foreign persons is 30% of the gross amount. This rate applies unless reduced by an applicable bilateral tax agreement. Wrong (A): 10% is not the default rate; it may apply under certain bilateral agreements for specific income types. Wrong (B): 15% is not the statutory default; some agreements reduce withholding to this level for dividends. Wrong (C): 21% is the U.S. corporate income tax rate, not the withholding rate on payments to foreign persons.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Services income sourcing with allocation
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Langford, a U.S. resident architect, earns $360,000 from designing a commercial building for a client in Singapore. Langford performs five months of work in Singapore and one month of work from her U.S. office. Under IRC Sections 861-862, evaluate how this services income is sourced.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$300,000 foreign-source and $60,000 U.S.-source, allocated based on where the services are performed", "Entirely foreign-source, because the project site and client are both in Singapore", "Entirely U.S.-source, because Langford is a U.S. resident", "$180,000 foreign-source and $180,000 U.S.-source, split equally between the two countries"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sections 861-862, personal services income is sourced based on where the services are physically performed. Five of six months were performed in Singapore (5/6 x $360,000 = $300,000 foreign-source) and one month in the United States (1/6 x $360,000 = $60,000 U.S.-source). Wrong (B): Income for services performed in the U.S. is U.S.-source regardless of the client's location; the one month of U.S. work cannot be ignored. Wrong (C): The residence of the service provider does not determine the source; where the work is performed controls. Wrong (D): The allocation is proportional to time spent in each location, not an equal split.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Branch vs. subsidiary loss utilization
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Hawkins Industries, a U.S. corporation, plans to open a new operation in Country Q expected to generate $900,000 in losses during each of the first two years before turning profitable in Year 3. Hawkins has substantial U.S. income from domestic operations. The CFO asks which structure — a foreign branch or a foreign subsidiary — will minimize Hawkins's aggregate U.S. tax liability over the three-year period. Evaluate which structure accomplishes this and why.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A foreign subsidiary, because subsidiary losses can be deducted against the parent's U.S. income in the year incurred", "A foreign branch, because branch losses flow through to the U.S. parent's return and reduce current U.S. taxable income during the loss years", "Either structure, because U.S. tax law treats branch and subsidiary losses identically for the parent corporation", "A foreign subsidiary, because the subsidiary can carry its losses forward and offset future subsidiary profits, permanently reducing the parent's U.S. tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): A foreign branch is not a separate legal entity, so its income and losses flow directly onto the U.S. parent's return. During the loss years, the $900,000 annual branch losses reduce Hawkins's current U.S. taxable income. A subsidiary's losses remain trapped at the subsidiary level and do not reduce the parent's U.S. income. Wrong (A): A foreign subsidiary is a separate taxpayer; its losses do not flow through to the U.S. parent's return. Wrong (C): Branches and subsidiaries have fundamentally different tax treatment — branch income and losses flow through currently, while subsidiary results are generally deferred. Wrong (D): While subsidiary loss carryforwards may offset future subsidiary profits, those profits are generally deferred from U.S. taxation anyway and do not reduce the parent's current U.S. tax.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Sale of personal property sourcing (seller's residence)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Donaldson Corp, a U.S. corporation, sells surplus office furniture to an unrelated buyer in Brazil for a $120,000 gain. The furniture was located at Donaldson's U.S. headquarters at the time of sale. Under IRC Sections 861-862, how is this gain sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source, because the buyer is in Brazil", "Split between U.S. and foreign source based on where negotiations occurred", "U.S.-source, because the seller is a U.S. resident and the property is non-inventory personal property", "Foreign-source, because the property will be exported outside the United States"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 865(a), gain from the sale of non-inventory personal property is generally sourced based on the residence of the seller. Donaldson is a U.S. corporation (U.S. resident), so the gain is U.S.-source. Wrong (A): The buyer's location does not determine the source of gain on non-inventory personal property. Wrong (B): The place of negotiation is not the sourcing rule for personal property sales. Wrong (D): The export destination does not change the sourcing; the seller's residence controls for non-inventory personal property.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Withholding agent responsibility
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under U.S. federal income tax rules, who bears the legal responsibility to withhold tax on U.S.-source payments to foreign persons?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The Internal Revenue Service collects the tax directly from the foreign payee", "The foreign payee must self-assess and remit the tax to the IRS", "The foreign payee's home country tax authority remits the withholding to the IRS", "The U.S. person making the payment acts as the withholding agent"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, the U.S. person making the payment to a foreign person is the withholding agent responsible for deducting and remitting the tax to the IRS. The withholding agent faces liability if it fails to withhold. Wrong (A): The IRS does not collect directly from foreign payees; the withholding mechanism places the obligation on the payer. Wrong (B): The withholding regime places the collection duty on the payer, not on self-assessment by the foreign payee. Wrong (C): Foreign tax authorities have no role in collecting U.S. withholding tax.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Personal property (equipment) rental income sourcing
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Trident Corp, a U.S. corporation, leases specialized drilling equipment to Zenon Ltd., a corporation in Norway. The equipment is shipped to Norway and used exclusively on Norwegian offshore platforms. Trident receives $600,000 annually under the lease. Under IRC Sections 861-862, how is this rental income sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source, because the leased equipment is used outside the United States", "U.S.-source, because the lessor is a domestic corporation", "Split between U.S. and foreign source based on Trident's domestic revenue percentage", "U.S.-source, because the lease was negotiated and signed in the United States"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sections 861-862, rental income from tangible personal property is sourced based on where the property is used. Because the drilling equipment is used exclusively in Norway, the rental income is foreign-source. Wrong (B): The lessor's residence does not determine the source of rental income from tangible personal property; the location of use controls. Wrong (C): Rental income sourcing does not use a revenue-allocation formula. Wrong (D): Where the lease agreement was negotiated is irrelevant to sourcing; the physical location where the property is used determines the source.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — CFC status requires 10% shareholders, not just any U.S. owners
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Ten unrelated U.S. individuals each own exactly 6% of the voting stock and value of Polaris Ltd., a foreign corporation (60% total U.S. ownership). The remaining 40% is held by non-U.S. persons. A junior analyst concludes that Polaris is a CFC because U.S. persons collectively own more than 50%. Evaluate this conclusion.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The analyst is correct because U.S. persons own 60% of voting power, exceeding the 50% threshold", "The analyst is incorrect because only U.S. persons owning at least 10% count as U.S. shareholders for the CFC test, and none of the ten individuals meets that threshold", "The analyst is correct because each U.S. person's ownership is aggregated regardless of individual percentage", "The analyst is incorrect because CFC rules only apply to corporations, not to individual shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 957(a), CFC status is determined by the collective ownership of U.S. shareholders, defined under Section 951(b) as U.S. persons owning at least 10% of voting power or value. None of the ten individuals owns 10% or more, so none qualifies as a U.S. shareholder. With zero qualifying U.S. shareholders, Polaris is not a CFC despite 60% total U.S. ownership. Wrong (A): Total U.S. ownership is irrelevant if none of the owners individually meets the 10% threshold to be a U.S. shareholder. Wrong (C): Ownership is not simply aggregated across all U.S. persons; each must independently meet the 10% threshold to count. Wrong (D): CFC rules apply to all U.S. persons — individuals, corporations, partnerships — provided each meets the 10% ownership threshold.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — PE requires permanence, not just any activity
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Riga Textiles, a Latvian corporation, sends a team of sales representatives to a U.S. industry conference for two weeks. The team displays samples, takes purchase orders from U.S. retailers, and returns to Latvia. Riga has no office, warehouse, or staff in the United States. Under the PE framework, does this conference attendance create a U.S. permanent establishment for Riga?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because taking purchase orders at a U.S. conference constitutes conducting business in the United States", "Yes, because the conference venue is a fixed place of business during the two-week period", "No, because a temporary presence at a trade event does not constitute the fixed and sustained business presence required for PE status", "No, but only because the sales team members are Latvian citizens, not U.S. persons"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the PE framework, a permanent establishment requires a fixed place of business with a degree of permanence. A temporary two-week presence at a conference does not meet this standard because it lacks the sustained, ongoing character required. Wrong (A): Conducting sales activity at a temporary event does not create a PE without a fixed and lasting place of business. Wrong (B): A conference venue used briefly is not a fixed place of business attributable to Riga; the location is temporary and not at Riga's disposal. Wrong (D): The citizenship of the sales team is irrelevant; the PE analysis turns on the nature and duration of the business presence, not the nationality of personnel.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Rental income sourcing by property location
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Atlas Realty, a U.S. corporation, owns an apartment complex in Tokyo generating $500,000 in annual rent and a warehouse in Houston generating $350,000 in annual rent. Both properties are leased to unrelated tenants. Under IRC Sections 861-862, how is each rental income stream sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both streams are U.S.-source because Atlas Realty is a domestic corporation", "Both streams are foreign-source because most of the rental income comes from the Tokyo property", "The Tokyo rent is U.S.-source and the Houston rent is foreign-source, based on the tenant's nationality", "The Tokyo rent is foreign-source and the Houston rent is U.S.-source, based on property location"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 861(a)(4), rental income is sourced based on the location of the property. The Tokyo property is outside the United States (foreign-source), and the Houston property is within the United States (U.S.-source). Wrong (A): The residence of the property owner does not determine rental income sourcing; property location controls. Wrong (B): The proportion of income from each property is irrelevant; sourcing depends on each property's location individually. Wrong (C): The tenant's nationality does not affect rental income sourcing; the situs of the property is determinative.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — CFC indirect ownership through foreign intermediary
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Avalon Corp, a U.S. corporation, owns 100% of ForeignHold Ltd., a foreign holding company. ForeignHold owns 60% of the voting stock of Praxis SA, a foreign operating corporation. Two unrelated non-U.S. persons own the remaining 40% of Praxis. Avalon's tax advisor needs to determine whether Praxis is a CFC. Evaluate Praxis's CFC status under the ownership attribution rules.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Praxis is a CFC because Avalon constructively owns 60% of Praxis through ForeignHold, making Avalon a U.S. shareholder owning more than 50%", "Praxis is not a CFC because Avalon does not directly own any Praxis stock", "Praxis is not a CFC because ForeignHold is a foreign corporation, and ownership through foreign entities is not attributed for CFC purposes", "Praxis is a CFC only if ForeignHold distributes dividends to Avalon during the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 958(a), stock owned by a foreign entity is constructively attributed to its U.S. shareholders. Avalon owns 100% of ForeignHold, which owns 60% of Praxis, so Avalon constructively owns 60% of Praxis. Avalon is a U.S. shareholder (owning at least 10%), and its 60% constructive ownership exceeds the 50% threshold, making Praxis a CFC. Wrong (B): Direct ownership is not required; the constructive ownership rules attribute stock held through intermediary entities. Wrong (C): The attribution rules under Section 958 explicitly apply through foreign entities — ownership is traced through the chain. Wrong (D): CFC status depends on ownership structure, not on whether dividends are actually distributed.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Source does not equal taxability
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$A junior tax associate writes in a research memo that because a U.S. corporation's income is classified as foreign-source under IRC Sections 861-862, the corporation does not owe U.S. federal income tax on that income. Is this statement correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because foreign-source income is exempt from U.S. tax for domestic corporations", "No, because U.S. corporations are taxed on worldwide income; source classification affects how double taxation is mitigated, not whether income is taxable", "Yes, because income sourcing determines both the country of taxation and the applicable rate", "No, but only because the income must first be reclassified as U.S.-source before it can be taxed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 61, U.S. corporations are taxed on worldwide income regardless of source. The classification of income as foreign-source is relevant to mitigating double taxation but does not exempt the income from U.S. tax. Source and taxability are related but distinct concepts. Wrong (A): Foreign-source income of a U.S. corporation is fully subject to U.S. tax; source classification does not create an exemption. Wrong (C): Income sourcing determines which country has primary taxing rights for mitigation purposes, but the U.S. taxes its residents on all income regardless. Wrong (D): The income does not need to be reclassified; it remains foreign-source and is still taxable because the U.S. uses a worldwide tax system.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — ECI determination when foreign corp has U.S. business but also unrelated investment
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Torino Automotive SpA, an Italian corporation, operates a car dealership in Miami generating $2,000,000 in sales profits. Separately, Torino holds a portfolio of U.S. municipal bonds generating $80,000 in interest, acquired through a New York brokerage account unrelated to the dealership. Torino's tax advisor must determine whether both income streams are taxed the same way. Evaluate the proper classification.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both items are exempt from U.S. tax because Torino is a foreign corporation with limited U.S. activities", "Both items are effectively connected income because Torino has a U.S. trade or business", "The dealership profits are effectively connected income taxed on a net basis, while the bond interest — if not connected to the dealership business — is analyzed separately as potential FDAP income", "The bond interest is ECI and the dealership profits are FDAP income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 864(c), income is ECI only if it is effectively connected to the foreign corporation's U.S. trade or business. The dealership profits are clearly ECI. The bond interest, acquired through an unrelated investment account, may not be connected to the dealership and would be analyzed separately. Having a U.S. trade or business does not automatically convert all U.S.-source income into ECI. Wrong (A): A foreign corporation with a U.S. trade or business is subject to U.S. tax on its ECI; foreign status does not create an exemption. Wrong (B): Not all income of a foreign corporation with a U.S. business is automatically ECI; each income item must be connected to the U.S. trade or business. Wrong (D): This reverses the classification; the dealership profits are ECI (connected to the U.S. business), not the investment income.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Foreign corp with only FDAP income taxed by withholding
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Sandoval SA, a Mexican corporation, has no U.S. office, employees, or business operations. Its only U.S. connection is a portfolio of U.S. corporate bonds generating $400,000 in annual interest. Sandoval's controller asks whether the company must file a U.S. corporate income tax return and pay tax on a net basis. Evaluate Sandoval's U.S. tax situation.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Sandoval must file a U.S. return and pay tax at the 21% corporate rate on the $400,000", "Sandoval is completely exempt from U.S. tax because it is a foreign corporation", "Sandoval must file a U.S. return reporting worldwide income because it earns U.S.-source interest", "Sandoval does not file a U.S. return; its U.S. tax obligation is satisfied through withholding at the source by the payers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, a foreign corporation with no U.S. trade or business earning only FDAP income (such as portfolio interest) has its U.S. tax obligation satisfied through withholding at the source. The U.S. payers deduct and remit the tax. No net-basis return filing is required because Sandoval has no effectively connected income. Wrong (A): Net-basis taxation at corporate rates applies to effectively connected income, which requires a U.S. trade or business. Portfolio bond interest without a U.S. business is FDAP taxed via withholding. Wrong (B): Foreign corporations are not exempt from U.S. tax on U.S.-source income; the withholding mechanism collects the tax. Wrong (C): A foreign corporation without a U.S. trade or business does not file a U.S. return reporting worldwide income; only ECI triggers net-basis filing.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Which persons owe U.S. tax on worldwide income
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Which of the following persons is subject to U.S. federal income tax on worldwide income, including income earned outside the United States?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A U.S. citizen, even one who lives and works entirely abroad", "A nonresident alien with no U.S.-source income", "A foreign corporation with no U.S. trade or business", "A nonresident alien who visits the United States for vacation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1, U.S. citizens are subject to tax on worldwide income regardless of where they reside or where the income is earned. A U.S. citizen living abroad still must report and pay U.S. tax on all income. Wrong (B): A nonresident alien with no U.S.-source income generally has no U.S. tax obligation; NRAs are taxed only on U.S.-source and ECI. Wrong (C): A foreign corporation without a U.S. trade or business is taxed only on U.S.-source FDAP through withholding, not on worldwide income. Wrong (D): A nonresident alien visiting for vacation who earns no U.S.-source income has no U.S. tax obligation from the visit alone.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Withholding applies to gross amount
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Orion Corp, a U.S. corporation, pays $500,000 in royalties to Kanto Ltd., a Japanese corporation with no U.S. trade or business. Kanto incurred $150,000 in expenses generating this royalty income. Kanto's advisor asks whether the U.S. withholding applies to the full $500,000 or only to the net $350,000. Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, on what amount is withholding computed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The net amount of $350,000 after deducting Kanto's expenses", "The gross amount of $500,000, because withholding on FDAP income is computed on the gross payment without deduction for the payee's expenses", "The net amount, because foreign payees are entitled to deduct expenses before withholding is applied", "Neither amount, because royalty payments between corporations are exempt from withholding"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, withholding on FDAP income paid to foreign persons is computed on the gross amount of the payment. Kanto's expenses are not deducted before withholding is applied. This is a fundamental distinction from net-basis taxation of effectively connected income, where deductions are allowed. Wrong (A): The payee's expenses are not deducted from the gross payment for withholding purposes. Wrong (C): Foreign payees receiving FDAP income cannot reduce the withholding base by their expenses; gross-basis taxation is a defining feature of the withholding regime. Wrong (D): Royalty payments to foreign persons without a U.S. trade or business are subject to withholding; they are not exempt.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Interest paid by U.S. borrower to foreign lender sourcing + withholding
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Magellan Corp, a U.S. corporation, borrows $8,000,000 from Banque Royale, a French bank with no U.S. operations. Magellan pays $480,000 in annual interest. The bank's advisor asks whether Magellan must withhold U.S. tax and how the interest is sourced. Evaluate Magellan's obligations.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Magellan has no withholding obligation because the loan funds are used outside the United States", "The interest is foreign-source because the lender is French, and no U.S. withholding applies", "The interest is U.S.-source because the borrower is a domestic corporation, and Magellan must withhold at the statutory rate on the gross payment", "Magellan must withhold only if Banque Royale fails to file a U.S. income tax return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 861(a)(1), interest paid by a domestic corporation is U.S.-source income. Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, Magellan must withhold U.S. tax at the statutory 30% rate on the gross interest payment to a foreign person with no U.S. trade or business. Wrong (A): Where loan proceeds are used does not change the sourcing of interest; the payer's residence controls. Withholding is required on U.S.-source interest paid to a foreign person. Wrong (B): Interest sourcing follows the payer's residence, not the lender's; because Magellan is a U.S. corporation, the interest is U.S.-source. Wrong (D): The withholding obligation arises from the payment itself, not from the lender's filing status; the payer must withhold regardless.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Deferral concept for foreign subsidiaries
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Corbin Industries, a U.S. corporation, wholly owns Ventura SA, a foreign subsidiary that earns $4,000,000 in Year 1 but pays no dividends. Corbin's new controller records $4,000,000 of subsidiary income on the consolidated U.S. tax return for Year 1. A senior tax manager reviews the return and identifies an error. Under general principles (assuming no anti-deferral provisions apply), what is the correct treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The controller is correct; U.S. corporations must include their foreign subsidiaries' earnings currently", "The controller is correct; deferral was eliminated for all CFC income in 2018", "The controller is correct; 100% ownership means the subsidiary is treated the same as a branch for tax purposes", "The income should not appear on Corbin's return until Ventura distributes it, because a foreign subsidiary's undistributed earnings are generally deferred from U.S. taxation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under general U.S. tax principles, a foreign subsidiary is a separate legal entity and its income is not included on the U.S. parent's return until repatriated through a dividend or other distribution. This deferral of tax on undistributed foreign subsidiary earnings is a fundamental feature of the U.S. international tax system, subject to specific anti-deferral exceptions. Wrong (A): U.S. corporations do not generally include their foreign subsidiaries' earnings currently; deferral applies until distribution. Wrong (B): Deferral was not eliminated entirely; certain anti-deferral provisions create current inclusions for specific income categories, but general deferral remains. Wrong (C): 100% ownership does not convert a subsidiary into a branch; the legal entity distinction controls regardless of ownership percentage.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — PE construction project duration threshold
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Bayern Bau GmbH, a German construction company, wins a contract to renovate a factory in Ohio. The project is estimated at 11 months. The applicable bilateral tax agreement provides that a construction project constitutes a permanent establishment only if it lasts more than 12 months. Midway through the project, the client requests design changes that extend the timeline to 15 months. Evaluate whether Bayern Bau has a U.S. permanent establishment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Bayern Bau has a PE because the project exceeds the 12-month threshold once the extension is included", "Bayern Bau does not have a PE because the original contract was for 11 months", "Bayern Bau has a PE only for the months beyond the original 11-month timeline", "Bayern Bau does not have a PE because construction-site PE rules only apply to new buildings, not renovations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the PE framework for construction projects, what matters is the actual duration of the project, not the originally contracted timeline. Because the project ultimately lasts 15 months, it exceeds the 12-month threshold and constitutes a PE for the entire duration. Wrong (B): The original contract duration is not determinative; the actual project duration controls. Wrong (C): PE status applies to the entire project from inception once the duration threshold is exceeded, not only to months beyond the threshold. Wrong (D): The construction-site PE rule applies to building sites, construction, and installation projects broadly, including renovations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — CFC status triggers U.S. taxation of certain income
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Nexus Corp, a U.S. corporation owning 100% of Solaris Ltd. (a foreign corporation), learns that Solaris qualifies as a CFC. Nexus's CFO asks what practical consequence CFC classification has for the company's U.S. tax position. Evaluate the primary tax effect of CFC status.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["CFC status has no tax consequence until Solaris distributes dividends to Nexus", "CFC status triggers current U.S. taxation of certain categories of the CFC's income to its U.S. shareholders, even without a distribution", "CFC status subjects Solaris itself to U.S. corporate income tax on all its worldwide income", "CFC status eliminates deferral entirely, causing all of Solaris's income to be taxed currently to Nexus"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): CFC classification triggers current U.S. taxation of certain categories of the CFC's income (such as specific anti-deferral inclusions) to U.S. shareholders, regardless of whether the CFC makes an actual distribution. This is the core consequence of CFC status. Wrong (A): CFC status creates current inclusions for certain income categories even without a dividend; this is the point of the anti-deferral rules. Wrong (C): The CFC itself is a foreign corporation and is not directly subject to U.S. corporate tax on worldwide income; the inclusion falls on the U.S. shareholders. Wrong (D): CFC status does not eliminate deferral entirely for all income; it triggers current inclusion only for certain specified categories of income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Gain on stock sale follows seller's residence
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Henley, a U.S. citizen residing in Dallas, sells shares of a publicly traded Swiss corporation for a $90,000 gain. The sale is executed through a U.S. brokerage account. Under IRC Section 865, how is this gain sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source, because the stock represents ownership in a Swiss corporation", "Foreign-source, because the Swiss stock exchange is the primary trading market", "U.S.-source, because the gain on the sale of non-inventory personal property is sourced based on the seller's residence", "Split equally between U.S. and foreign source because one party is in each country"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 865(a), gain from the sale of non-inventory personal property — including corporate stock — is generally sourced based on the residence of the seller. Henley is a U.S. citizen residing in the United States, making the gain U.S.-source. Wrong (A): The country of incorporation of the corporation whose stock is sold does not determine the source of gain on a stock sale; the seller's residence controls. Wrong (B): The location of the trading market is not the general rule for sourcing gain on personal property; the seller's residence is determinative. Wrong (D): Stock sale gain sourcing does not use an equal-split method; it follows the seller's residence.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Gain on depreciable personal property used in U.S.
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Helsinki Corp, a Finnish corporation, operates a manufacturing plant in Michigan and sells a piece of factory equipment used exclusively at that plant for a $200,000 gain. The equipment was depreciated over several years against Helsinki's U.S. effectively connected income. Helsinki has no other U.S. assets. Evaluate how this gain is sourced.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source, because Helsinki is a foreign corporation", "Split between U.S. and foreign source based on Helsinki's worldwide income ratio", "Foreign-source, because gain on personal property follows the seller's residence rule", "U.S.-source, because the depreciable property was used in Helsinki's U.S. trade or business"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 865(c), gain on the sale of depreciable personal property is sourced based on where the depreciation deductions were allocated. Because the equipment was depreciated against U.S. effectively connected income and used in a U.S. business, the gain is U.S.-source. This overrides the general seller-residence rule for personal property. Wrong (A): The seller's foreign residence does not control when the property was used and depreciated in a U.S. trade or business. Wrong (B): Depreciable property gains are not allocated by a worldwide income ratio; they follow the depreciation allocation. Wrong (C): The general seller-residence rule for personal property is overridden for depreciable property by the prior-depreciation-allocation rule.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — No withholding on payments for goods
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, which type of payment to a foreign person is generally NOT subject to U.S. withholding tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Payments for the purchase of tangible goods", "Interest on a loan to a U.S. corporation", "Royalties for use of intellectual property", "Dividends paid by a U.S. corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sections 1441-1442, U.S. withholding applies to fixed, determinable, annual, or periodical (FDAP) income such as interest, dividends, rents, and royalties. Payments for the purchase of tangible goods are not FDAP income and are not subject to withholding. Wrong (B): Interest paid to a foreign person is FDAP income subject to 30% statutory withholding. Wrong (C): Royalties paid to a foreign person are FDAP income subject to withholding. Wrong (D): Dividends paid by a U.S. corporation to a foreign person are FDAP income subject to withholding.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: easy / L2 — Foreign corp with only portfolio investments has no ECI
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Bellavista SA, an Argentine corporation, holds a portfolio of U.S. publicly traded stocks and bonds. It has no U.S. office, no employees in the United States, and does not conduct any business operations in the country. Bellavista receives $320,000 in U.S.-source dividends and interest during the year. Under U.S. tax law, is this income considered effectively connected income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because all U.S.-source income of a foreign corporation is automatically ECI", "No, because effectively connected income requires a connection to a U.S. trade or business, and passive portfolio investments alone do not constitute a trade or business", "Yes, because owning U.S. securities creates a permanent establishment in the United States", "No, but only because the total income is below the $500,000 threshold for ECI classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 864(c), effectively connected income requires that the foreign corporation be engaged in a U.S. trade or business and that the income be connected to that business. Passive portfolio investing in publicly traded securities does not constitute a U.S. trade or business, so Bellavista's dividends and interest are not ECI. Wrong (A): Not all U.S.-source income is ECI; the income must be connected to a U.S. trade or business conducted by the foreign corporation. Wrong (C): Holding portfolio investments does not create a permanent establishment; PE requires a fixed place of business or dependent agent. Wrong (D): There is no $500,000 threshold for ECI classification; the test is whether the income is connected to a U.S. trade or business, regardless of amount.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L2 — Services entirely in U.S. are U.S.-source
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Patel, a nonresident alien management consultant, travels to the United States and performs all of a $180,000 consulting engagement at a client's offices in Chicago over two months. The client is a subsidiary of a corporation based in India. Under IRC Sections 861-862, how is this income sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source, because Patel is a nonresident alien", "Foreign-source, because the client's parent company is Indian", "U.S.-source, because all of the services are physically performed in the United States", "Split between U.S. and foreign source because the payer's parent is foreign"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sections 861-862, compensation for personal services is sourced based on where the services are physically performed. Because Patel performs all work in the United States, the entire $180,000 is U.S.-source income regardless of Patel's residency or the client's parentage. Wrong (A): The service provider's residency does not determine the source of services income; where the work is performed controls. Wrong (B): The nationality or location of the client's parent corporation is irrelevant to services income sourcing. Wrong (D): Because all work is performed in one country, there is no basis for splitting the income between sources.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Foreign branch vs subsidiary: legal entity distinction
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What is the key legal distinction between a foreign branch and a foreign subsidiary of a U.S. corporation that determines their different federal income tax treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A foreign branch operates in a developing country while a foreign subsidiary operates in a developed country", "A foreign branch generates only services income while a foreign subsidiary generates only sales income", "A foreign branch must have fewer than 50 employees while a foreign subsidiary has no employee limit", "A foreign branch is part of the U.S. corporation itself, while a foreign subsidiary is a separate legal entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): The fundamental distinction is that a foreign branch is not a separate legal entity — it is an extension of the U.S. corporation. A foreign subsidiary is a separately incorporated entity. This determines their tax treatment: branch income flows directly to the U.S. parent's return, while subsidiary income is generally deferred until distributed. Wrong (A): The country's economic classification has no bearing on whether an operation is structured as a branch or subsidiary. Wrong (B): Both branches and subsidiaries can earn any type of income; the distinction is legal entity status, not income type. Wrong (C): Employee count does not distinguish branches from subsidiaries; the difference is whether the operation is a separate legal entity.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
